      "sha256": "9ec73981eccf481719e067cab5d1639445de5edfa71abb31da6a6cad1c367986",
      "kind": "figure",
      "alt": "Fig. 1. An example of a Jupyter notebook. (1) A custom collaborative extension for users to share their notebook. (2) A notebook cell that contains code to import libraries and load dataset. (3) An output of a shared data frame. (4) A markdown cell that contains narrative text. (5) An output of a visualization"
    },
    {
      "path": "figures/wang-how-data-scientists-cscw2019/figure-002-p019.png",
      "node": "1461:0",
      "page": 19,
      "bbox": [
        45.112998962402344,
        82.68160247802734,
        440.8890075683594,
        215.83999633789062
      ],
      "sha256": "e80df22eea17c3655f9f75d70c58ba9dcc9ef97ea44cb234e46dfa9316db42a2",
      "kind": "figure",
      "alt": "Fig. 2. Overview of how participants iteratively explore the house price prediction task in 15-minute intervals. Participants in the shared condition tended to switch between phases more frequently. The initial attempts at modeling occurred earlier in the shared condition."
    },
    {
      "path": "figures/wang-how-data-scientists-cscw2019/figure-003-p020.png",
      "node": "1491:0",
      "page": 20,
      "bbox": [
        43.827999114990234,
        82.68000030517578,
        444.4231872558594,
        283.2879943847656
      ],
      "sha256": "6df82d44f311447ca6c31fa183986eb861f2453bc322b88d20a871cbbb2ed582",
      "kind": "figure",
      "alt": "Figure 3. Stacked response distributions on a seven-point scale from strongly agree to strongly disagree, comparing shared and non-shared notebooks. Combined strongly-agree and agree percentages, shared versus non-shared, are: following conversations 80% versus 79%; effective team communication 67% versus 77%; awareness of teammates’ subtasks 80% versus 71%; awareness of teammates’ technical issues 56% versus 40%; fair workload 70% versus 54%; all teammates fully engaged 77% versus 84%; enough work 82% versus 88%; waiting for others 14% versus 17%; enjoyment of teammates 82% versus 71%. Most responses are positive except the negatively worded waiting question, where disagreement is common. Bars also show somewhat agree, neutral and three levels of disagreement."
    },
    {
      "path": "figures/wang-how-data-scientists-cscw2019/figure-004-p022.png",
      "node": "1561:0",
      "page": 22,
      "bbox": [
        91.96299743652344,
        82.6780014038086,
        394.0429992675781,
        274.3580017089844
      ],
      "sha256": "77071d54cd6b273e9477fe72ad6c0b1204183c3f3d2342643b77ce2f4ffe3cea",
      "kind": "figure",
      "alt": "Figure 4. Group S3 notebook begins with a Pre-processing and cleaning plan: replace discrete values with indices; remove samples with too many missing features; normalize continuous variables; compute correlations or otherwise select features. Two code cells below implement count_nans, printing missing-value counts per column, and label_encoding, converting object-valued columns to categorical codes. Written subgoals coordinate the team’s later code work."
    },
    {
